{"hierarchy":{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents","modules":[{"projects":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol","sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Mark-a-protocol-as-mockable","kind":"task"},{"kind":"task","reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Create-the-system-under-test"},{"kind":"task","reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Stub-return-values-with-when"},{"kind":"task","reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Verify-the-interaction"}]}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Getting-Started"},{"projects":[{"sections":[{"kind":"task","reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments#Match-any-or-exactly-one"},{"kind":"task","reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments#Match-with-predicates-and-ranges"},{"kind":"task","reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments#Capture-and-inspect-arguments"}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments"}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Argument-Matching"},{"projects":[{"sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Return-computed-values","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Order-your-stubs","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Run-side-effects-with-do","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Drive-callback-based-APIs","kind":"task"}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing"},{"sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Assert-call-counts","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-order-across-mocks","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-thrown-errors","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Wait-for-calls-that-havent-happened-yet","kind":"task"},{"kind":"task","reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Verify-property-reads-and-writes"},{"kind":"task","reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Debug-failing-verifications"}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions"}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Advanced-Stubbing-and-Verification"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Swift-Testing-Integration","projects":[{"sections":[{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration#Control-what-unstubbed-requirements-return","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration#Mock-static-requirements-safely","kind":"task"},{"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration#Use-the-rest-of-Swift-Testings-vocabulary","kind":"task"}],"reference":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration"}]}],"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents","doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/$volume","doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Getting-Started"]]},"sections":[{"estimatedTimeInMinutes":10,"kind":"hero","backgroundImage":"intro-first-mock.png","title":"Mock Your First Protocol","chapter":"Getting Started","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In this tutorial you’ll test a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"text":" that depends on a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PricingService"},{"text":" — without writing a mock class by hand. You’ll mark a protocol with ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Mockable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", let the macro generate the mock, stub what its method returns, and verify that your code actually called it.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Along the way you’ll meet the two verbs that power every SwiftMocking test: ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"when"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" to arrange behavior, and ","type":"text"},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"verify"}]},{"type":"text","text":" to assert it happened."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"image":"intro-first-mock.png"},{"kind":"tasks","tasks":[{"title":"Mark a protocol as mockable","contentSection":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every mock starts life as a plain protocol describing a dependency. The ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Mockable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" macro expands that protocol into a ready-to-use mock class at compile time, so the protocol remains your single source of truth — when it changes, the mock changes with it.","type":"text"}]}],"mediaPosition":"trailing","media":"chapter-getting-started.png","kind":"contentAndMedia"}],"anchor":"Mark-a-protocol-as-mockable","stepsSection":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Consider a store that needs prices. Pricing rules live behind a protocol so tests never depend on real pricing logic. Create ","type":"text"},{"code":"PricingService.swift","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with the protocol:","type":"text"}]}],"media":null,"runtimePreview":null,"code":"01-1-1-pricing-service.swift","caption":[],"type":"step"},{"runtimePreview":null,"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Now make it mockable: import "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiftMocking"},{"type":"text","text":" and annotate the protocol with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Mockable"},{"type":"text","text":":"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"media":null,"type":"step","caption":[],"code":"01-1-2-pricing-service.swift"},{"runtimePreview":null,"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"That single annotation is all the macro needs. It generates "},{"code":"MockPricingService","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" next to your protocol — the equivalent of writing this by hand. You never create or edit this file yourself:"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"media":null,"type":"step","caption":[],"code":"01-1-3-generated-mock.swift"},{"type":"step","runtimePreview":null,"caption":[],"media":null,"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Notice the generated class carries "},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"two overloads","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" of ","type":"text"},{"code":"price(for:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". The ","type":"text"},{"code":"ArgMatcher<String>","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" version returns an ","type":"text"},{"code":"Interaction","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — calling it, as in ","type":"text"},{"code":"mock.price(for: .any)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"describes"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" a call for stubbing or verification and never runs your code. The plain ","type":"text"},{"code":"String","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" version is the real protocol requirement: calling it records the invocation and returns the stubbed value. That duality is the whole trick — the same syntax describes a call and performs it.","type":"text"}]}],"code":null}]},{"anchor":"Create-the-system-under-test","contentSection":[{"media":"intro-first-mock.png","kind":"contentAndMedia","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"With the mock generated, build the class you actually want to test. "},{"code":"Store","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" takes any "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PricingService"},{"text":" — in production a real implementation, in tests the mock.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"mediaPosition":"trailing"}],"title":"Create the system under test","stepsSection":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Create "},{"code":"Store.swift","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". Start with the state the store owns: the items it has registered and the price it recorded for each."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"media":null,"type":"step","code":"01-2-1-store.swift","runtimePreview":null,"caption":[]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Inject the dependency. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"text":" holds ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"any PricingService"},{"text":" rather than a concrete type — that existential is what lets a mock stand in for the real implementation without ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"text":" knowing the difference.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"media":null,"type":"step","code":"01-2-2-store.swift","runtimePreview":null,"caption":[]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Add the behavior under test. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"register(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":" appends the item and asks the pricing service what it costs — the single call your test will stub and then verify."}]}],"media":null,"type":"step","code":"01-2-3-store.swift","runtimePreview":null,"caption":[]}]},{"anchor":"Stub-return-values-with-when","contentSection":[{"kind":"contentAndMedia","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"An unstubbed method can’t answer on its own. ","type":"text"},{"code":"when","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" arranges behavior: you describe the call with a matcher — here the literal ","type":"text"},{"code":"\"apple\"","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — and declare what it returns.","type":"text"}]}],"mediaPosition":"trailing","media":"chapter-getting-started.png"}],"title":"Stub return values with when","stepsSection":[{"caption":[],"code":"01-3-1-store-tests.swift","type":"step","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Create ","type":"text"},{"code":"StoreTests.swift","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and wire up the two objects: the mock, and the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Store","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" that receives it. Nothing is stubbed yet — the mock exists, but it has no answers.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"media":null,"runtimePreview":null},{"caption":[],"code":"01-3-2-store-tests.swift","type":"step","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Arrange the behavior. Each "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"when"},{"type":"text","text":" describes a call — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"price(for: \"apple\")"},{"text":" — and pins the value it returns. The bare string literal is sugar for ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".equal(\"apple\")"},{"text":", so each stub answers only its own item.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"media":null,"runtimePreview":null},{"code":"01-3-3-store-tests.swift","media":null,"runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Act, then assert. Registering both items drives the real "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" logic, and the prices it recorded are exactly the ones you stubbed."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"caption":[]},{"code":null,"media":null,"runtimePreview":null,"type":"step","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Run the test. The store called "},{"code":"price(for:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" twice — once per registered item — and each call was answered by the matching stub. No real pricing logic was ever involved."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"caption":[]}]},{"stepsSection":[{"runtimePreview":null,"media":null,"code":"01-4-1-store-tests.swift","type":"step","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Add a verification to the test. The "},{"code":".any","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" matcher matches every call regardless of the item, and "},{"code":".called(2)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" asserts exactly two invocations:"}]}],"caption":[]},{"caption":[],"media":null,"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Run the test again — it still passes, and now it also fails loudly if the store ever stops consulting the pricing service."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"step","runtimePreview":null,"code":null},{"code":null,"caption":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"You’ve completed the full mocking loop: "},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"generate","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" with ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Mockable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"arrange","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" with ","type":"text"},{"code":"when","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"act"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" on your type, ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"assert","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" with ","type":"text"},{"code":"verify","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Confirm the assertion has teeth. Temporarily change "},{"code":".called(2)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".called(3)"},{"type":"text","text":" and the test fails at that line with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Unfulfilled call count. Actual: 2"},{"type":"text","text":" — the message reports what really happened, not just that something went wrong. Change it back before moving on."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"step","runtimePreview":null,"media":null}],"anchor":"Verify-the-interaction","contentSection":[{"kind":"contentAndMedia","mediaPosition":"trailing","media":"intro-verifying.png","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Asserting output is half the story; interaction tests also assert ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"that a dependency was used, and how"}]},{"text":". ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"verify"},{"text":" takes the same call description as ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"when"},{"text":" and asserts how often it happened.","type":"text"}]}]}],"title":"Verify the interaction"}]},{"action":{"overridingTitleInlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Get started"}],"isActive":true,"overridingTitle":"Get started","type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/matching-arguments"},"featuredEyebrow":"Tutorial","title":"Match Arguments Precisely","kind":"callToAction","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Stubbing "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\"apple\""},{"type":"text","text":" and verifying "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".any"},{"type":"text","text":" is just the beginning. Real tests need finer control: "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"any"}]},{"type":"text","text":" amount, amounts "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"under"}]},{"type":"text","text":" 100, codes "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"in"}]},{"text":" a range, collections ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"of a certain size","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" — or some predicate only you can express.","type":"text"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol"]}],"metadata":{"categoryPathComponent":"Table-Of-Contents","category":"SwiftMocking","role":"project","title":"Mock Your First Protocol"},"kind":"project","identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"references":{"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/verifying-interactions#Wait-for-calls-that-havent-happened-yet":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"You’ve verified counts and matched arguments. This page completes the toolkit: counting calls with the full matcher vocabulary, asserting a call happened "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"never","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":", proving a mock was "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"untouched","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":", verifying thrown errors, waiting for calls that arrive from another task, distinguishing property reads from writes, and — the signature feature — verifying the "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"order","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" of calls across multiple mocks."}],"kind":"section","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Wait-for-calls-that-havent-happened-yet","role":"pseudoSymbol","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/verifying-interactions#Wait-for-calls-that-havent-happened-yet","type":"section","title":"Wait for calls that haven't happened yet"},"01-1-3-generated-mock.swift":{"syntax":"swift","fileName":"MockPricingService.swift","fileType":"swift","highlights":[],"type":"file","content":["\/\/ Generated by the @Mockable macro — you never write this yourself.","#if DEBUG","class MockPricingService: Mock, @unchecked Sendable, PricingService {","    func price(for item: ArgMatcher<String>) -> Interaction<String, None, Int> {","        Interaction(item, spy: super.price)","    }","    func price(for item: String) -> Int {","        return adapt(super.price, item)","    }","}","#endif"],"identifier":"01-1-3-generated-mock.swift"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/Table-Of-Contents/Advanced-Stubbing-and-Verification":{"title":"Advanced Stubbing and Verification","abstract":[],"kind":"article","url":"\/tutorials\/table-of-contents\/advanced-stubbing-and-verification","role":"article","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Advanced-Stubbing-and-Verification"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/mocking-your-first-protocol#Verify-the-interaction":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"In this tutorial you’ll test a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" that depends on a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PricingService"},{"type":"text","text":" — without writing a mock class by hand. You’ll mark a protocol with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Mockable"},{"type":"text","text":", let the macro generate the mock, stub what its method returns, and verify that your code actually called it."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Verify-the-interaction","kind":"section","role":"pseudoSymbol","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Verify-the-interaction","type":"section","title":"Verify the interaction"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/Table-Of-Contents/Argument-Matching":{"role":"article","abstract":[],"kind":"article","type":"topic","title":"Argument Matching","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/Table-Of-Contents\/Argument-Matching","url":"\/tutorials\/table-of-contents\/argument-matching"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/mocking-your-first-protocol#Mark-a-protocol-as-mockable":{"url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Mark-a-protocol-as-mockable","title":"Mark a protocol as mockable","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"In this tutorial you’ll test a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" that depends on a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PricingService"},{"type":"text","text":" — without writing a mock class by hand. You’ll mark a protocol with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Mockable"},{"type":"text","text":", let the macro generate the mock, stub what its method returns, and verify that your code actually called it."}],"kind":"section","role":"pseudoSymbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Mark-a-protocol-as-mockable","type":"section"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/dynamic-stubbing#Run-side-effects-with-do":{"url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Run-side-effects-with-do","title":"Run side effects with do","abstract":[{"text":"Fixed return values only go so far. Real dependencies ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"compute"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":": a calculator multiplies its operands, a sync engine mutates state, a network service delivers results through callbacks.","type":"text"}],"kind":"section","role":"pseudoSymbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/dynamic-stubbing#Run-side-effects-with-do","type":"section"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/mocking-your-first-protocol#Stub-return-values-with-when":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"In this tutorial you’ll test a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store"},{"type":"text","text":" that depends on a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PricingService"},{"type":"text","text":" — without writing a mock class by hand. You’ll mark a protocol with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Mockable"},{"type":"text","text":", let the macro generate the mock, stub what its method returns, and verify that your code actually called it."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Stub-return-values-with-when","kind":"section","role":"pseudoSymbol","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/mocking-your-first-protocol#Stub-return-values-with-when","title":"Stub return values with when","type":"section"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/verifying-interactions#Debug-failing-verifications":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/verifying-interactions#Debug-failing-verifications","kind":"section","title":"Debug failing verifications","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/verifying-interactions#Debug-failing-verifications","role":"pseudoSymbol","type":"section","abstract":[{"text":"You’ve verified counts and matched arguments. This page completes the toolkit: counting calls with the full matcher vocabulary, asserting a call happened ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"never"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", proving a mock was ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"untouched"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", verifying thrown errors, waiting for calls that arrive from another task, distinguishing property reads from writes, and — the signature feature — verifying the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"order","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" of calls across multiple mocks.","type":"text"}]},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/testing-integration#Control-what-unstubbed-requirements-return":{"kind":"section","type":"section","abstract":[{"text":"Every test so far has been an ordinary ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Test","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". This page covers what Swift Testing adds on top: ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"traits"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":", which scope behavior to a test or a whole suite.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/SwiftMocking\/tutorials\/SwiftMocking\/testing-integration#Control-what-unstubbed-requirements-return","role":"pseudoSymbol","url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/testing-integration#Control-what-unstubbed-requirements-return","title":"Control what unstubbed requirements return"},"doc://SwiftMocking/tutorials/SwiftMocking/matching-arguments#Match-with-predicates-and-ranges":{"url":"\/tutorials\/swiftmocking\/matching-arguments#Match-with-predicates-and-ranges","title":"Match with predicates and ranges","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Stubbing "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\"apple\""},{"text":" and verifying ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".any"},{"type":"text","text":" is just the beginning. Real tests need finer control: "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text"